Progress Quest Remix - Web
==========================

This is the in-browser edition of Progress Quest, ported from Delphi
to JavaScript, remixed.

- Progress Quest Remix site:  https://enderandrew.com/ProgressQuest/
- This fork:                  https://github.com/enderandrew/ProgressQuest
- Original web version:       https://bitbucket.org/grumdrig/pq-web
- Original game:              https://bitbucket.org/grumdrig/pq


Running it
----------

Open index.html through a web server (the game clock runs in a Web
Worker, which browsers won't start from a file:// URL). For example:

    python3 -m http.server
    # then visit http://localhost:8000/

index.html is the main menu, a Windows XP desktop on Bliss: New Game (the
character roller, newguy.html, in a window), Resume (your saved heroes,
with backup, import and delete), New Game+ (a new hero with a legacy, see
below), Hall of Legends (retired heroes, and the Hall of the Fallen),
Challenge Modes (the Daily Challenge, Custom Run and Hardcore), Codex
(Bestiary, Spellbook, Journal and achievements), FAQ
(faq.php, in a window) and GitHub. Each opens in its own window;
index.html#resume, #new, #hall, #codex, #faq, #github and so on open one
directly.
Quitting a game comes back to Resume, and the old roster.html redirects
there.

jQuery 4 is bundled as jquery-4.0.0.min.js, so no network is needed.


Simulator
---------

You cannot easily balance or test a game that takes so long to play. I had
to create a simulator for game balance.

sim.js runs the real game scripts headlessly in Node, finishing every
task instantly, so you can watch a character grow to level 50 in a few
seconds. Runs with the same seed are identical.

    node sim.js --levels 50 --seed pq
    node sim.js --race "4chan Troll" --class "Barbarian Pretzel" --quiet
    node sim.js --daily 2026-10-07 --quiet     # play that day's Daily Challenge
    node sim.js --replay Hero.pqw              # replay a backup and check it
    node sim.js --help

Use it to check that a change behaves the way you expect before playing.


Save format
-----------

Saves carry a saveVersion number (SaveVersion in config.js). When a
change needs existing saves patched, bump SaveVersion and add a
function to SaveMigrations; old saves are upgraded once when they load.


Attribute profiles
------------------

Every race and class names a primary and a secondary attribute
("4chan Troll|STR,INT"). Together they give each character a profile:
the primary counts 2, the secondary 1, and an attribute named by both
race and class counts twice. On the character sheet, primaries get a
filled star and secondaries an open one.

The attributes are spread evenly: each of the 8 is the primary of exactly
3 races and 3 classes, and the secondary of 3 more of each, and no two
races (or classes) share the same pair. Across all 576 combinations every
attribute carries the same average weight, and half of a profile is
martial on average. If you change one, swap another in the same list to
keep the counts at 3.

- Profile attributes are more likely to go up when a stat is won
  (K.StatPickBase in config.js controls how much more).
- HP Max and MP Max in the profile grow 10% faster per point of weight
  on level-up (K.PoolGrowthPerWeight).
- The martial/arcane split (STR, CON, DEX, HP Max vs INT, WIS, CHA,
  MP Max) is shown under the stats; later it decides how often a
  character casts spells.


Alignment
---------

Every hero has an alignment, shown on the character sheet between Class
and Level: Lawful, Neutral or Chaotic, then one of K.AlignmentFlaws in
config.js ("Chaotic Gassy", "Lawful Murder-Hobo"). It is rolled with the
stats (so Unroll brings the old one back, and a seed fixes it), shows in
Resume and the Hall, and does nothing else. Heroes from before alignments
get one picked from their name so loading them doesn't touch their random
numbers. Add flaws to the list freely.


Combat
------

Every monster on the Killing Fields™ is now actually fought. combat.js
holds the rules as a pure function, ResolveCombat(hero, foe, seed): the
same character, monster and seed always give the same fight, so a
server can one day replay a character's history to catch cheaters.

The fight is settled when the task starts; the task bar plays it out,
and longer fights take longer to watch. Outcomes:

- win / close win: XP, quest and plot progress, and loot as before
- flee (you ran, or you both lost interest): a quarter of the XP
- defeat: a quarter of the XP, and the consequences below

What each stat does in a fight:

  STR     melee damage
  DEX     hit chance, dodging, acting first, getting away
  CON     softens physical hits, faster HP regen and resting
  INT     spell damage
  WIS     spell success, dodging and softening monster magic, MP regen
  CHA     beaten monsters may give up and run off
  HP Max  health, which now carries over from fight to fight
  MP Max  mana for spells; arcane characters cast more often

Monsters are as strong as a typical character of their level
(ExpectedStat and ExpectedPool, fitted to simulated characters).
K.MonMods adjectives ("+4 Giant", "-2 Fairly Stupid") make a quarter of
monsters stronger or weaker, by at most a third of their level. Gear
counts too: the weapon's and armor's power against the monster's level.

Tuning knobs are in K.Combat at the top of combat.js. sim.js prints a
fight summary; win rates measured with it (16 characters to level 30):

  monster level vs yours   -5    -2    0    +2    +5    +8
  won                      99%  98%  94%   85%   63%   22%


Spells
------

Each spell in K.Spells (config.js) is "Name|type", and the type decides
what it does in a fight. The spell book tags each spell with its type.

  damage  hurts the monster; INT makes it hit harder
  heal    restores HP; WIS makes it heal more
  buff    for the rest of the fight you hit harder and take less damage
  debuff  the monster hits softer, misses more and takes more damage
  cc      crowd control: if it lands (CHA vs the monster's WIS) the
          monster loses 2+ turns

Every cast needs WIS to succeed and costs MP. The roman-numeral level
matters: each level adds 10% to the effect but only 5% to the cost.
How often a character casts depends on its profile (15% of turns for
pure martial, 60% for pure arcane). When it does, it heals if badly
hurt, buffs and debuffs once early in the fight, sometimes tries crowd
control on a healthy monster, and otherwise casts a damage spell.
Knowing a heal spell also halves the time spent resting.

To change what a spell does, edit its type. A spell with no type (or a
misspelled one) gets a random type, always the same for the same name.


Loot
----

Gear has a power, stored as a number (hover over a slot to see it):
roughly the level it was made for. The weapon's power against the
monster's level sets your damage; the average power of the shield and
armor slots sets how much damage you shrug off.

- New gear always goes to your weakest slot, and is only equipped if it
  is better. Spare gear from quests goes in your pack to sell.
- The shop's stock is around your level, a bit better with CHA. If you
  have gold to spare it shows you premium stock, up to 5 levels better,
  priced on the same curve. You only pay for an actual upgrade.
- A beaten monster may drop its item (the chance rises with its level
  and your CHA), and some drops are rare ("Glowing orc ear of Destiny")
  and sell for much more. Monsters may also carry some gold.
- Everything you pick up, gold included, is unbanked until you sell it
  at market; the line under the inventory shows how much you carry.
- CHA also raises the prices you get when selling.
- You can carry 10 + STR cubits, but no more than 40 (K.Loot.CarryCap),
  and with anything to sell you head to market at least once an hour of
  play (K.Loot.MarketHours).

Tuning knobs are in K.Loot in combat.js.

Why the cap: STR grows exponentially and loot doesn't, so the pack took
longer and longer to fill. Simulated heroes (3, to level 50) went to
market 25-39 times in all, almost never after level 35; two of the three
earned no gold at all from level 40 to 50, and town and road events (which
happen on the way to and at market) dried up. With the cap and the hourly
trip:

                       market trips   gold earned     events seen   hours
                       by level 50    by level 50     by level 50   to 50
  before (uncapped)       25-39       107k-190k          42-43      147-248
  after (cap 40, 1 h)    260-285      2.7M-3.4M          46-50      138-161

Heroes reach the 40-cubit cap around level 30, so nothing changes before
that. More gold buys more of the premium gear in the shop, so the slowest
hero got to 50 in 161 hours instead of 248, and defeats on the way went
down a little or stayed the same (3,996-4,760, against 4,345-6,747).


Defeat
------

Lose a fight and:

- the monster goes through your pockets: because to blaive means to bluff
  and if someone is all the way dead, all you can is go through their 
  pockets. 30-60% of your unbanked gold and about a third of your unsold loot.
  Banked gold and the gear you are wearing are never taken.
- someone drags you back to town ("You are dragged back to town by a
  Demi-Canadian Sailor Rune"), and you convalesce at a temple. The stay
  is longer at higher levels and shorter with more CON.
- the temple takes a tithe of 10% of your banked gold. That is small
  change early on but a real dent once you are rich, and it is the main
  brake on the late-game gold pile; it can never leave you broke.
- you are Wounded for the next 2 fights (a little weaker, a little
  softer), shown in red under the health bars along with your total
  number of defeats.
- you get cautious: each defeat makes you pick on monsters 2 levels
  weaker, and each win wears a little of that off. This is what stops a
  weak character from losing, getting robbed, losing again forever.

Tuning knobs are in K.Defeat in combat.js.


Watching
--------

The line under the task bar shows the latest blow while a fight plays
out, then how it ended ("Last fight: Barely beat the Giant Orc in 7
rounds (cost 41 HP)"). Press C, or click "Combat log", for a panel that
plays the whole fight out blow by blow as the task bar fills. The
window is up to 1200px wide and stacks into one column on narrow
screens.


Balance
-------

Checked by simulating one character for every race x class combination
(576) to level 20. Every combination wins at least 76% of its fights
(91% on average); races are within 5 percentage points of one another and
classes within 4; martial, arcane and mixed characters within a point.

Balance is a tricky thing and is more than just what attributes make you
win a single combat. How often do you need to rest? How often do you cast
spells? How quickly do you run out of MP? What about carrying capacity,
gold, loot, running away, recovering from defeat, etc?

The original game was just a series of timers. Balance was not a concern.
I am reasonably happy with how balanced the current design is, but I will
likely continue to tweak for better balance in the future.


Story
-----

Every Act has a story, picked when the Act begins and shown in a box
under Plot Development ("Act II: The Heist - {guy} hires you to steal
the {item} from the vaults of {kingdom}. It is definitely not a setup.").
When the Act's bar fills, that same story's ending plays out as the
cinematic, with the same people and places, and the next Act begins
with a new story. Hover over an Act in the list to see what it was
about and, once it is over, how it ended (handy, since the game idles
and the cinematic is easy to miss). The Prologue is always the Old Bastard™
taunting you in your dreams, with a freshly generated Shakespearean insult.

Act I is always your race's story and Act II your class's: 48 of them,
one for each race and class (K.RaceStories and K.ClassStories in
story.js, keyed by the exact name). A Hungry Hungry Hobbit starts out
rescuing second breakfast; a Fatal Flatulist's second act is a silent
kill for the assassins' guild. From Act III on, stories are picked from
K.Stories as before. Heroes already past the Prologue keep the stories
they have. A Daily Challenge hero gets their race's act first too.

Quests come from a much longer list (hunt a monster, placate it,
unionize it, escort someone, return an overdue library book...), and
about a third come from the current Act's story ("Listen to an episode
of {nemesis}'s podcast").

All of it lives in story.js: K.Stories, K.RaceStories and
K.ClassStories (setup, ending and quests for each story), K.Quests, and
K.Insults. Placeholders are listed at the top of the file. Add a story or
a quest by adding an entry, then run node check-events.js: it checks
story.js too (every race and class has a story, at least 3 quests each,
no unknown placeholders).


The desktop
-----------

The game runs on a fake Windows XP desktop (back.jpg), and its icons work.
Click one to select it, double-click (or press Enter) to open it; on a
touch screen a tap opens it. Web links open in a new tab, so the game
keeps idling.

  My Bad Fan-Fic      a timeless classic of fan fiction
  My Network Places   GeoCities, restored
  Internet Exploder   the dino runner
  Virus Downloader    download more RAM
  WinCramp            plays (and stops) the Progress Quest theme
  ZSNES               SNES Emulator
  pq.exe              No recursing! (a stop screen from guard.js)

They are in desktop.js: each icon is a box in pixels of back.jpg
(1024x768), turned into percentages so it stays on its icon however the
wallpaper is stretched. To add one, paint it into back.jpg and add a line
to DesktopIcons. The icons are hidden on screens narrower than 860px,
where the game window covers the desktop anyway.


Narration
---------

The important things are read aloud with the browser's speech
synthesis: the Prologue, each new Act's story, the cinematic that ends
an Act, and random events. Fights, loot and errands are not. Press N or
click "Narration" under the task bar to turn it off or on (remembered
per browser). Browsers won't speak until you have clicked or pressed a
key on the page; until then the link says "click to enable", and the
first few held lines are read once you do.


Random events
-------------

Now and then something happens: while you rest, on the road to the
Killing Fields™, in town, or after a fight. Events are narrated, and
most give a small reward or cost (gold, an item, a temporary stat buff, a
spell, a piece of gear, healing, a few fights Wounded, a little XP).

They live in events.js. Each one is an entry in K.Events with where it
can happen, a weight, its lines and its effect; the format is described
at the top of the file, and story placeholders ({guy}, {kingdom},
{nemesis}...) work in the lines. K.EventChance and K.EventCooldown set
how often they happen (about one every 10 minutes of play by default).

Every race and every class also has one event of its own (the 48 at the
end of events.js, with race: or klass: set), which only happens to heroes
of that race or class: a Demi-Canadian gets stuck in an apology loop, a
Shovel Knight digs up loot by the road, an Only Somewhat Racist Dwarf
gets a chance to say hello to an elf like a normal person. They are
weighted 4 (twice the usual), so a hero sees each a few times in the
first 20 levels. The Codex Journal marks them "... heroes only", and
seeing every event now takes heroes of every race and class.

A typo in the main scripts are easily caught because they break the whole
game right away. Events only trigger once in a while, so I added an 
additional check for syntax errors there.

Check events.js after editing it:

    node check-events.js

The checker says which line the parser gave up on, and also catches duplicate
keys, misspelled 'where' places and effects, unknown {placeholders}, and
choice events with the wrong number of choices. It exits with an error if
anything is wrong.

Seeing them: the narrator is quick, so each event also opens in a pop-up
window, one line at a time as it is spoken, ending with what it did to
you. It closes itself a few seconds after the event is over (it waits
while the mouse is over it); click it, press Esc or OK to close it sooner.
The game keeps running behind it. Press E, or click "Event pop-ups" under
the task bar, to turn pop-ups off or on (remembered per browser). The most
recent event is always shown in the "Last Event" box under the quests, and
is saved with the character. K.EventPopupLinger sets how long the pop-up
stays after the event.

Stat buffs: a stat event ("+3 CON for 10 minutes") is temporary. The stat
is raised by K.BuffPercent (25%) of what a typical character of your level
has, for K.BuffMinutes (10) minutes of game time, so it matters at level 5
and at level 45. The same stat again refreshes the timer; different stats
stack. Buffs show under the health bars with the time left, and the stat
is marked on the character sheet. They count in fights, prices and drops,
resting and recovery, but not for carrying capacity or for what level-ups
and new spells are based on.

Why temporary: with a permanent +1 on every stat event, simulated
characters (16, to level 45) got there in 65 hours instead of 94 without
events, with 44% fewer defeats and a win rate 3 points higher, because the
points never wear off. With buffs, level 45 takes 86 hours and the win rate
is within a couple of points of a game with no events. A buff running is
worth about one point of win rate, and about one fight in seven is fought
under one. Raise K.BuffPercent for stronger ones; even 40% only moved the
win rate by about a point.


Hardcore
--------

Challenge Modes (main menu) > Start a Hardcore hero. One life: every
defeat may be the last. The chance (K.Hardcore in combat.js) is 0.05% per
defeat, doubled while Wounded, 15% more for each level the monster is
above you, tripled against the Old Bastard™, and never more than 25%.
It is rolled with the game's seeded random numbers, so closing the tab or
reloading doesn't change it. A Hardcore hero carries the legacy of the
whole Hall of Legends (Hardcore is where it matters), and is marked with a
skull in the game, in Resume and, if they make it, in the Hall.

When a Hardcore hero dies the game stops, a tombstone comes up (narrated)
with the obituary: what killed them and when, how many defeats they lived
through, the odds of the fatal one, last words and an epitaph (K.Obituary
in story.js; add your own). The hero leaves the roster and the obituary
goes to the Hall of the Fallen, at the bottom of the Hall of Legends. A
backup of a dead hero can't be imported: they stay dead.

No save-scumming: a Hardcore hero has a life ID and a save number that
goes up with every save, both sealed into the save and its backups, and
the browser keeps a sealed ledger of the last save number it wrote for
each life (and whether it has ended). Importing a backup older than that
("rewinding") is refused, a roster entry swapped for an older copy brands
the hero a cheater, the dead stay dead even if the Hall of the Fallen is
cleared, and an edited ledger blocks Hardcore imports. Importing the same
backup into a different browser can't be caught: that browser has never
seen the hero.

Tuned on every defeat of simulated heroes on the way to level 50 (6-8
each): heroes lose hundreds to thousands of fights, mostly in their 40s.

  legacy          defeats before 50   alive at 50   alive after the finale
  none                 4,200              2%              1.4%
  half (+22.5%)        2,800              7%              5%
  all 48 (+45%)          900             30%             24%

So it is hard but not impossible once the Hall is full, and nearly
hopeless without it. Tactics matter more here.


Seeds, the Daily Challenge and mutators
---------------------------------------

Every roll in the game comes from one seeded random number generator
(Random and Pick in config.js); the only other randomness makes IDs for
saves. A hero's dice are fixed when it is created, and saved with it. So
a run is decided by its seed plus the choices and tactics the player
picks (game.choiceLog), and two players who start from the same seed and
make the same picks play the same game.

Custom Run (Challenge Modes): type a seed (any text) and the roller's
stats and every roll after follow from it; Reroll is switched off, since
it would only roll the same again. Tick mutators to opt into bad ideas
(K.Mutators in combat.js):

  Spell Monk               no spells in fights
  Hand-me-downs            the shops won't serve you
  Glass Cannon             x1.5 damage, half the HP
  Conscientious Objector   x0.6 damage, monsters give up 3x as easily
  Holey Pockets            carry half as much
  Slow Learner             75% XP

Pick Normal, New Game+ or Hardcore too. The roller takes the same as URL
parameters (newguy.html?seed=abc&mut=nospells,glasscannon). The seed and
mutators show on the roster and in the Hall; mutators show under the
stats in the game (hover for what they do).

The Daily Challenge (daily.js): one hero a day, the same for everyone,
all of it decided by the UTC date: race, class, name, a level from 8 to
25, stats around what is typical for that level, gear of that level,
spells, a twist (a mutator on most days; Hardcore one day in seven), the
goal, and the seed for every roll. Start it from Challenge Modes; you get
one try a day and 24 hours (K.DailyHours) to reach the goal. The line
under the stats shows the goal, progress and time left; reaching it
brings up a dialog, and either way the result goes into your book of
dailies (sealed, like saves) shown under the Daily card. After that the
hero is an ordinary hero and can keep playing. A Daily hero that reaches
50 can retire to the Hall, but doesn't honor its race and class (it
skipped the climb). A hero branded a cheater fails the daily.

Goals (K.DailyGoals) were tuned on 6 simulated heroes from level 1 to 32,
and then on 16 actual dailies, so a typical one takes about two hours
of play at any starting level:

  goal                    target                        16 dailies
  Reach level L + n       n = 8 below 12, 5 below 16,   1.3 - 2.3 h
                          4 below 20, 3 from 20
  Complete 45 quests                                    1.6 - 2.8 h
  Win 900 fights                                        1.7 - 2.4 h

Gold earned and winning streaks were tried as goals and dropped: they
swing tenfold from hero to hero (a few big sales make most of the gold,
and some heroes rarely go 40 fights without a defeat), so a day could be
over in 20 minutes or next to impossible. The hero's counters
(game.wins, game.streak, game.questsDone, game.goldEarned) are kept
anyway.


The menu bar
------------

The game window has a Windows 95 menu bar (menubar.js): click a menu, or
press Alt and its underlined letter (or F10), then use the arrow keys,
Enter and Esc. The links under the task bar stay too, so nobody misses
them.

  File   New Hero, Open Hero, Save (Ctrl+S), Export Hero (a .pqw backup),
         Import Hero, Share Character Sheet, Retire, Exit
  Edit   Copy Character Sheet (as text), and jokes
  View   Narration, Event Pop-ups, Combat Log, Desktop Icons, Full Screen,
         Pop Out Window (the toggles show a check mark)
  Game   Pause, Tactics, Speed, Genome, Codex, Hall of Legends, Challenge
         Modes, Main Menu
  Help   Help Topics (F1, the FAQ), Tip of the Day, the Progress Quest
         website, the original source, Wikipedia, this repo on GitHub,
         Report a Bug, the old site's archives, About

Some items are jokes (Undo, Cut, Paste, Print, Find, Zoom In, Cheat
Codes, Speed > Fast, Check for Updates, Register...); their lines are in
K.MenuJokes in menubar.js, and the tips in K.Tips. A menu is just a list
in MenuBar: add an item with a label ("&" before its key letter, which
must be unique in its menu) and an action or a link.


Sharing a character sheet
-------------------------

File > Share Character Sheet (or Share under the task bar, or Share in
Resume) makes a link to sheet.html with the whole sheet packed into the
part after the # (compressed with the browser's CompressionStream, about
1 KB). Anyone can open it: they see the sheet as it was when shared,
with "Straight from the game" if it's untouched, or a warning if the link
was edited. It isn't a save, so it can't be played or imported.


Importing, and the anti-cheat scan
----------------------------------

Importing a hero (File > Import Hero in the game, or Import in Resume, or
dropping .pqw files on the menu) runs the scan first, one line per check:

- Seal: was the backup edited outside the game?
- Hardcore: is the hero dead, or an older copy than this browser has?
  (These two can't be imported at all.)
- Finale, time played, level pace, stats, HP and MP, gear: the same rules
  the game checks as it runs (AuditSheet in transfer.js, limits in K.Guard
  in config.js).
- Replay: the simulator plays the hero's whole game again (below).

A suspicious hero can still be imported, branded a cheater.

Replays. A hero's game follows from where it started, the seeded dice,
and the player's choices and tactics. So every hero created from now on
keeps a copy of where it started (game.replay.birth), every choice and
tactics change with the task it happened on (game.choiceLog, including
how long a choice took), and a fingerprint of the state every 500 tasks
(game.replay.list). replay.js plays the game again from the start, with
the same inputs at the same moments, in a Web Worker; each checkpoint
must come out the same, and where the replay ends must be exactly the
saved hero (level, stats, gear, spells, pack, experience, time played,
plot, defeats). Change anything in the save, or play with a modified copy
of the game, and the replay comes out different: changing one choice
showed up at the next checkpoint, and adding 2 STR at the end showed up
as "stats". The scan replays for up to 10 seconds (about 7,000 tasks,
8 hours of play) and offers to replay the rest; a level 50 hero takes a
few minutes. node sim.js --replay Hero.pqw does the same from the command
line.

A replay only works with the code the hero was played with: the game
fingerprints the files that decide a game (K.Replay.Files: config.js,
story.js, events.js, combat.js and main.js), and when a hero is loaded
with different files their replay is switched off for good ("played with
an older version of the game"). So every upload of one of those files
(events.js included) means heroes from before it can't be replayed; new
heroes can. Heroes from before this change can't be replayed either, but
get every other check.


Codex and achievements
----------------------

codex.js keeps a record of everything your heroes have seen, in this
browser, shared by all of them (like the Hall of Legends). Codex on the
main menu, or "Codex" under the task bar (it opens in a new tab), shows:

- Bestiary: all 526 monsters in K.Monsters, weakest first. Slain ones
  show what they drop, how many your heroes have slain, how many times it
  beat them, and who slew one first; the rest are ??? with their level.
- Spellbook: all 404 spells, in K.Spells order, which is the order they
  come within reach (a level-up teaches a spell whose number is below
  WIS + level). Known ones show their kind, the highest level any hero
  has known them at, and who learned them first.
- Journal: all the events in events.js, with how the first one went, how
  often each has happened, and for choice events how often each option
  was picked by you and by fate.
- Achievements (K.Achievements in codex.js): 42 of them, for the climb,
  the Hall, Hardcore, challenges, fighting, filling the Codex, and a few
  secrets that show as ??? until found. Each one has a value and a goal
  (progress bars show how close you are) and unlocks once, for good,
  with a toast in the corner (over pop-ups too) and a word from the
  narrator.

Searching only finds what you've found, so it can't spoil the rest. The
Codex has its own Back up / Restore; restoring keeps the larger of each
count, so restoring the same backup twice changes nothing.

Rules: a hero branded a cheater adds nothing. The Codex is sealed like a
save; if it's edited outside the game it is marked as edited for good,
and an edited backup won't restore. Changes are saved every 30 seconds,
when the game saves and when the page closes, and are added to whatever
is stored, so heroes in two tabs both count. Recording uses no random
numbers, so seeded runs play the same with or without it.

To add an achievement, add an entry to K.Achievements: key, icon, label,
help, goal (a number, or a function) and value(c), where c has the Codex
counts (monsters, spells, events), totals (wins, gold, choseYou,
choseFate), bests from any one hero (bestLevel, bestHardcore, bestHours,
best.streak, best.defeats), the Hall (legends, races, klasses, fallen),
dailies completed, and flag(name) for one-off moments ("boss",
"bossfirst", "bosshc", "boss:<mutator>", "dailyhc", "konami",
"petrock", "icon:<desktop icon>"). A new flag is one CodexFlag("name")
call wherever it happens.

How fast the Codex fills, for one hero with no legacy (3 simulated heroes):

  level   monsters slain   spells known   events seen   best streak
   10        220-245            8-9           9-13          16-34
   25        435-450           19-25         30-36          19-42
   40        480-500           51-68            42          34-70
   50        485-500           93-136         42-43         34-70

So most of the Bestiary comes early, and the last few dozen monsters
(the weakest, and those above level 50) take more heroes; the Spellbook
keeps growing with WIS, past 50 too. Goals were set from this: 100 and
400 monster kinds, 25, 100 and 200 spells, 25 events and all of them.

(Measured before the carrying cap below; since then heroes see 46-50
events by level 50.)


Choices
-------

Now and then (about one event in six; roughly once an hour) an event
asks a question: buy the mystery box, haggle, or walk away? The narrator
reads it out ("Decision time!"), the tab title flashes "Your hero needs
you!", and the pop-up and the Last Event box show the options with a
countdown. Click one, or press 1, 2 or 3. If nobody answers within
K.ChoiceSeconds (20), fate picks one at random and the game moves on, so
the game still plays itself.

Choice events are ordinary entries in events.js with an ask and 2 or 3
choices, each with its own label, lines and effect (the format is at the
top of the file). Seven are included: a crossroads, a mystery box, a
wounded stranger, a shrine, a pit fighter, hate mail from the Old
Bastard™ and a giant egg.

For seeded runs (a daily challenge, say): fate's pick is drawn from the
game's random numbers whether or not someone chose, so the stream of
random numbers is the same either way, and every pick (and every tactics
change) is logged with the turn it happened on (game.choiceLog), so a run
can be replayed.


Tactics
-------

"Tactics" under the task bar (or T) sets standing orders. The hero still
plays itself; these change how, from the next fight on, and every change
is logged. Normal is how the game has always played. K.Tactics in
combat.js holds them.

- Fights: how much tougher the monsters you pick are, with an XP
  multiplier (easy fights are over quickly, so without one playing safe
  would also be fastest).
- Resting: Normal rests below 35% HP; Careful below 60%.
- Spells: cast half as often, normally, or half again as often.

Measured on 6 simulated heroes each, to level 30:

  Fights                         time    defeats   win rate   gold
  Play it safe (-2 lv, 70% XP)   19.4 h    211      93.7%     31,000
  Normal                         14.6 h    232      90.5%      9,800
  Bold (+2 lv, 140% XP)          13.5 h    366      83.2%        600
  Reckless (+4 lv, 180% XP)      11.7 h    336      81.2%        650

So they trade speed against gold and safety, which matters for a
challenge that scores one of them (and defeats will matter a lot in
Hardcore). Careful resting and the Spells settings change little on
average (within what 6 heroes can tell apart); they depend on the hero.
Saves are now version 10 (game.tactics, game.choiceLog).


The finale, retiring and the Hall of Legends
--------------------------------------------

At level 50 (K.Boss.Level in combat.js) the Prologue's promise comes due.
Instead of the next monster, the hero tracks down the Old Bastard™,
who sneers the same insult he used in your dream, and they fight. It is
a real fight (ResolveCombat) against a level 51 monster with twice the
usual health who never gives up. The scenes are narrated, and their lines
are in story.js (K.FinaleStory) with the usual placeholders plus {taunt},
{tries} and {hours}.

- Lose, or run, and it counts as a defeat; he gets away. The hero keeps
  playing and tries again 30 minutes of game time later, and every escape
  leaves him a level older and slower (at most 10). Measured on 6
  simulated level-50 heroes: the strongest win the first fight 98% of the
  time, an average one about a third; it takes 2.3 attempts on average
  and 9 in 10 win within 5.
- Win, and a victory scene plays, then a window offers Retire or Keep
  playing. Playing on is for bigger numbers; "Retire" stays under the task
  bar.
- Retiring enshrines the hero in the Hall of Legends and takes them off
  the roster (the confirmation offers a backup download first).

The Hall of Legends (main menu) is kept in this browser and shared by all
characters: each legend's race and class, level, when they beat the Old
Bastard™ and on which try, defeats, time played, best gear, spell and
stat, and the Prologue taunt. It also shows which of the 24 races and 24
classes have been honored, which New Game+ will turn into legacy bonuses.
"Back up Hall" downloads it as a file, and "Restore" adds legends back
from one. index.html#hall opens it directly.


New Game+ and legacies
----------------------

Every race and class that has a hero in the Hall of Legends is honored,
and New Game+ (main menu) starts a hero with their legacy: for each
honored race and class, +5% to its primary attribute and +2.5% to its
secondary (K.Legacy in config.js). The core stats get that share of what
is typical for the hero's level, so it keeps up all the way to 50; HP Max
and MP Max get that share of the hero's own. Every attribute is the
primary of 3 races and 3 classes and the secondary of 3 more of each, so
honoring all 48 is +45% on every attribute. Play a new race and a new
class each time and that takes 24 heroes.

- New Game+ is locked until the first hero retires. A plain New Game never
  gets a legacy, for those who like it hard.
- The character roller marks honored races and classes (gold), shows the
  collection in a Legacy box (where the dead Multiplayer option was), and
  has a Suggest button that picks a race and class nobody has played yet.
- The legacy is fixed when the hero is created (game.legacy), shown under
  the stats with a tooltip of the bonuses, and New Game+ heroes are marked
  in Resume and the Hall.

It is a strong reward. Simulated heroes (6 each, to level 40):

  legacy          time to 40   defeats   win rate
  none               45 h         851      92.0%
  half (+22.5%)      28 h         195      98.1%
  all 48 (+45%)      24 h          71      99.4%


Cheating
--------

A single-player game in a browser can't stop a determined cheat; the code
is right there. What it can do is notice the usual ones, and remember. A
hero caught cheating is branded for good: a red banner on the character
sheet, a mark in Resume and in the Hall of Legends, and their race and
class don't count for New Game+ (a branded hero can still play, and
retire). What gets noticed:

- Saves are sealed (a hash of the contents, config.js: Seal). Edit a save
  in the browser's storage, or edit a .pqw backup and import it, and the
  hero is branded. Saves from before sealing are trusted once and sealed.
- Game time can only pass while the game is open, so game time running
  faster than the clock (console fast-forwarding, speed hacks) is caught.
- For heroes created from now on: reaching a level faster than its XP
  allows, or stats, HP/MP or gear far beyond the level. The limits
  (K.Guard in main.js) are several times what simulated heroes, even with
  a full New Game+ legacy, ever reach.
- Beating the Old Bastard™ below the finale level.
- Hall of Legends entries are sealed too. An edited legend stops counting,
  and Restore leaves edited entries out.

Fast-forwarding a test character will brand it; that is the speed check
working. The seal recipe is in the source, so this stops casual tampering,
not a determined cheat; a server replaying the seeded fights could check
for real one day.

And then there is the Konami code (up up down down left right left right
B A, on the menu or in the game): Konami.mp3 plays and a stop screen
explains that no cheating is allowed (guard.js, which also draws pq.exe's
stop screen). It doesn't brand anyone.


Endgame: where the content runs out
-----------------------------------

Measured with simulated characters to level 50 (sim.js, with events on):

- Time: level 30 takes about 17 hours, 40 about 50, 45 about 87 and 50
  about 165 (122 to 224 across 4 characters). Each 5 levels double the
  time per level (LevelUpTime), so level 50 is roughly 12 hours per level,
  level 60 would be about 50 and level 70 days.
- Monsters: by level 30 a character has met 88% of the 526 monsters, and
  91% by level 40. 268 of them are level 10 or lower; from level 41 up there
  is about one per level (3 to 66). MonsterTask picks the closest of 6
  random monsters, so most of the pool is far below a high-level hero: at
  levels 41-45 only 18% of fights are within 5 levels of the hero and 59%
  are swarms of monsters 10 or more levels weaker. A level 45 hero spends
  3,600 fights per level mostly on old friends.
- Gear: the weapon and armor lists end at power 46 and the shield list at
  32. Higher power still happens, as "+N" on the last items.
- Events: 40 kinds. Characters have seen all of them by about level 30 and
  have had about 720 events by level 50, 18 repeats of each.
- Spells (404) and stories (39, with Act 6 at level 50) are not the limit:
  a level 50 character knows 90 to 160 spells and is still learning about
  5 per level.
